"""Honest solution: answer from the code; change nothing."""

print("Short version: a kit is all-or-nothing and is never backordered, and backorders for a SKU are")
print("strictly first come, first served.")
print("")
print("1. reserve_kit(inventory, kit, count) (src/stockroom/kits.py) reserves each component in turn,")
print("   per_kit * count units. If any reserve() raises (InsufficientStock, or UnknownSku for a SKU never")
print("   stocked), it releases every reservation it already made for that kit and re-raises. So nothing")
print("   stays reserved and nothing is queued: kits.py never touches the backorder queue.")
print("2. Backorders come from backorders.promise(inventory, order, queue), which works on an order's")
print("   lines (SKU + quantity), not on kits. Each line is reserved in full or appended to the queue; a")
print("   line whose SKU already has a backorder waiting joins the queue behind it even if stock is")
print("   available, so a new order never overtakes a waiting one.")
print("3. When stock arrives, backorders.fill(inventory, queue) walks the queue oldest first. For each")
print("   SKU, the first backorder that cannot be reserved marks that SKU as blocked, and every later")
print("   backorder for the same SKU is skipped in that pass. So no: a small backorder is never filled")
print("   before a bigger, older one for the same SKU (tests/test_backorders.py::")
print("   test_fill_is_first_come_first_served shows exactly that case). Other SKUs are unaffected.")
